===Etymology 1===

====Verb====
{{head|en|verb form}}

# {{infl of|en|ride||spast}}
# {{lb|en|now|colloquial|nonstandard}} {{past participle of|en|ride|nocat=1}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1662|page=26|title=A Saint Or a Brute{{...}}|author=John Baxter|url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=GvQ7AQAAMAAJ|passage=No doubt many a journey you have '''rode''' and gone, and many a hard daies labour you have taken, and ſharpened perhaps with care and grief{{...}}}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1827|origyear=1780|title=The Journal of the Rev. Francis Asbury{{...}}|author=Francis Asbury|volume=II|url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=F74DAAAAYAAJ|passage=We dined at Martin's, and then came on to father Low's: we have '''rode''' but eight miles this day. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|date=5 May 2014|isbn=9781559367738|publisher=Theatre Communications Group|author=Eric Bogosian|title=100 (monologues)|page=100|url=https://books.google.co.nz/books?id=FPjoCAAAQBAJ|passage=I have '''rode''' with the Kings, man, and I have '''rode''' with the best! I know what the truth is, and the truth is that I count and you don't.}}

===Etymology 2===

====Verb====
{{en-verb|rodes|roding|roded|roded}}

# {{lb|en|ornithology}} Of a male [[woodcock]], to fly back and forth over the edge of a woodland while calling; to perform its, typically [[crepuscular]], mating [[flight]].
#* {{quote-book|1=en|year=1886|translator=H.L. Br&aelig;kstad|author=w:Peter Christen Asbj&oslash;rnsen|title=Folk and Fairy Tales|page=85|passage="When the sun rises we shall have some splendid play. Only hear the woodcock, how he is '''roading'''; he expects fine weather."}}

===Etymology 3===

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|nautical}} The [[line]] from a [[vessel]] to its [[anchor]].

=====Synonyms=====
* {{l|en|warp}}

