==Middle English==

===Etymology 1===
{{etymid|enm|cross}}
From {{inh|enm|ang|rōd}}, from {{inh|enm|gmw-pro|*rōdu}}, from {{inh|enm|gem-pro|*rōdō}}. The final vowel is generalised from the Old English inflected forms.

====Alternative forms====
* {{alter|enm|rod|rood|roude|rud|rude}}

====Pronunciation====
* {{IPA|enm|/ˈroːd(ə)/}}
* {{rhymes|enm|oːd|s=1}}

====Noun====
{{enm-noun|pl=rodes|pl2=roden}}

# A [[cross]] or [[gibbet]]
# The cross on which [[Christ]] was [[crucified]], and derived uses such as:
## A [[crucifix]]
## Christlike [[torment]], [[suffering]], or [[tribulation]], as in "an oðer '''rode''' to berene" (another cross to bear)
# A [[rod]], [[pole]], or [[bar]]
# A [[quarter]] of an [[acre]]; a [[rood]]
# In place names: a woodland {{m|en|clearing|id=treelessness}}.

=====Descendants=====
* {{desc|en|rood}}
* {{desc|sco|rude|ruid}}
* {{desc|en|Rhode|Rhodes}}

